<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<section xml:id="sec-6-8-tostring-equals">
  <title>Printing and Comparing Objects: toString and equals</title>

  <p>
    One of the defining benefits of object-oriented design is <em>extensibility</em>: the 
    ability to add functionality to an existing system without modifying the original code. 
    Object-oriented languages provide extensibility primarily through a mechanism called 
    <em>inheritance</em>, which we will explore in detail in a future chapter. We can gain 
    an immediate glimpse of the benefits of inheritance now, however, by adding methods to 
    our classes that allow us to print, compare, and concatenate our objects naturally.
  </p>

  <p>
    All classes in Java are children of a universal parent class called <c>Object</c>. The 
    <c>Object</c> class provides default behaviors that all Java classes automatically 
    inherit. Let's examine two of those foundational behaviors: object comparison and 
    converting an object to a <c>String</c>.
  </p>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-8-1-object-comparison">
    <title>Object Comparison</title>

    <p>
      As you know, if you compare two object references with the equality operators (<c>==</c> 
      or <c>!=</c>), it is the stack values<mdash/>the memory addresses of the objects on the 
      heap<mdash/>that are compared. Take a moment to form a mental picture of the stack and the 
      heap after this code executes:
    </p>

    <program language="java">
      <code>
Point p1 = new Point(3.0, 4.0);
Point p2 = p1;
Point p3 = new Point(p1);
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      You should be picturing two distinct <c>Point</c> objects in the heap. Both hold 
      coordinate values <m>(3.0, 4.0)</m>, but the first object is referenced by both <c>p1</c> 
      and <c>p2</c>, while the second object is referenced by <c>p3</c>. The first object was 
      created with <c>new</c> and its address stored in <c>p1</c>. The assignment statement on 
      the next line makes <c>p2</c> an alias to that same memory address. On the third line, 
      another memory block is allocated on the heap, the copy constructor is invoked (copying 
      coordinates <m>3.0</m> and <m>4.0</m>), and that new address is stored in <c>p3</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      If we test these references with the equality operator:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <c>p1 == p2</c> evaluates to <c>true</c> because both variables hold the exact same 
          heap memory address.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <c>p1 == p3</c> (or <c>p2 == p3</c>) evaluates to <c>false</c> because, even though 
          their coordinate fields hold identical values, they point to separate heap allocations. 
          The values stored on the call stack are different.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      One of the methods that all classes inherit from <c>Object</c> is the <c>equals</c> method. 
      You have encountered it when comparing <c>String</c> instances (where <c>==</c> checks 
      reference identity rather than text equality). We can invoke the inherited <c>equals</c> 
      method on our <c>Point</c>, <c>Rectangle</c>, or <c>ElectricVehicle</c> objects directly. 
      Unfortunately, the default implementation inside <c>Object</c> simply returns the result 
      of the <c>==</c> operator! Therefore, calling <c>p1.equals(p3)</c> still returns <c>false</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      However, we can customize the <c>equals</c> method by <em>overriding</em> it. To override 
      a method means to write a custom version of an inherited method for a specific class. 
      Java will automatically invoke your custom version on objects of your class, rather than 
      the inherited default. To override a method, the method signature must match the original 
      signature exactly.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is an established, professional pattern for writing <c>equals</c> methods:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Place the <c>@Override</c> annotation above the method header so the compiler verifies 
          that your signature matches the parent method.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Declare the exact signature: <c>public boolean equals(Object obj)</c>. Note that the 
          parameter type is <c>Object</c>, not your class type, and is conventionally named <c>obj</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Check for reference identity using <c>this == obj</c> as an immediate fast path.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Verify that the incoming argument is not <c>null</c> and belongs to the exact same class.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Cast the <c>Object</c> parameter to your class type and compare all significant fields.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      <xref ref="listing-point-equals"/> illustrates an <c>equals</c> method for the <c>Point</c> 
      class adhering to this pattern:
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing-point-equals">
      <caption>An <c>equals</c> method for the <c>Point</c> class</caption>
      <program language="java">
        <code>
    /**
     * Indicates whether some other object is "equal to" this Point.
     * Two points are considered equal if they are the exact same instance,
     * or if the other object is also a Point with identical x and y coordinates.
     *
     * Precondition: None (obj may be null or any object type).
     * Postcondition: Returns true if obj is an instance of Point with coordinates
     *                matching this point; false otherwise. The state of this Point
     *                and obj are unchanged.
     *
     * @param obj the reference object with which to compare
     * @return true if this Point is equal to the obj argument; false otherwise
     */
    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        boolean equal = false;
        if (this == obj) {
            // Check if obj is the exact same instance as this
            equal = true;
        } else if (obj != null &amp;&amp; obj.getClass() == this.getClass()) {
            // If the object exists and is of the same class, compare fields
            Point other = (Point) obj;
            equal = (Double.compare(this.x, other.x) == 0 
                     &amp;&amp; Double.compare(this.y, other.y) == 0);
        }
        return equal;
    }
        </code>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      Let's trace how this method functions:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          On line 16, we initialize a boolean result flag to <c>false</c>, adhering to the 
          single-exit design pattern.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          On line 17, we check <c>this == obj</c>. If both variables hold the exact same heap 
          address, they are aliases representing the exact same instance, so <c>equal</c> is 
          set to <c>true</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If they are not the same physical instance, the <c>else if</c> branch executes. It 
          uses short-circuit evaluation: if <c>obj</c> is <c>null</c>, evaluation halts and 
          avoids calling methods on a null reference. If <c>obj</c> is non-null, <c>obj.getClass() == this.getClass()</c> 
          verifies that the object was instantiated from the <c>Point</c> blueprint (rather than 
          being a <c>String</c> or a <c>Scanner</c>).
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Once verified, line 23 safely casts <c>obj</c> to <c>Point other</c>, giving the 
          compiler access to <c>Point</c>'s coordinate fields.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Line 24 compares coordinates using <c>Double.compare(this.x, other.x) == 0</c>. Using 
          <c>Double.compare</c> is the professional Java standard for floating-point values 
          because it properly handles IEEE 754 edge cases, such as distinguishing <c>-0.0</c> 
          from <c>+0.0</c> and treating <c>Double.NaN</c> consistently.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      When writing an <c>equals</c> method for an object composed of other objects (such as 
      <c>Rectangle</c> holding a <c>Point topLeft</c>), you must perform a <em>deep comparison</em>. 
      Compare reference fields using their respective <c>.equals()</c> methods rather than 
      <c>==</c>. For example, in <c>Rectangle.equals</c>:
    </p>

    <program language="java">
      <code>
equal = (Double.compare(this.width, other.width) == 0)
        &amp;&amp; (Double.compare(this.height, other.height) == 0)
        &amp;&amp; (this.topLeft.equals(other.topLeft));
      </code>
    </program>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-8-2-overriding-tostring">
    <title>Overriding <c>toString</c></title>

    <p>
      Have you ever observed the cryptic output produced when printing an object directly? 
      If you pass an object reference to <c>System.out.println(p1)</c> before overriding its 
      display method, the console displays output such as:
    </p>

    <console>
      <output>
Point@1dbd16a6
      </output>
    </console>

    <p>
      This string is produced by the default <c>toString</c> implementation inherited from 
      <c>Object</c>. It consists of the class name (<c>Point</c>), an at-sign (<c>@</c>), and 
      the object's unsigned hexadecimal <em>hash code</em>, an integer generated from the 
      object's memory address that serves as a temporary identifier.
    </p>

    <p>
      Java implicitly invokes an object's <c>toString</c> method whenever an object is passed 
      to <c>System.out.print</c> or <c>println</c>, and whenever an object is concatenated with 
      a <c>String</c> using the <c>+</c> operator:
    </p>

    <program language="java">
      <code>
System.out.println("The value of p1 is: " + p1);
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      yields:
    </p>

    <console>
      <output>
The value of p1 is: Point@1dbd16a6
      </output>
    </console>

    <p>
      Just like <c>equals</c>, we can override <c>toString</c> to return a meaningful, 
      human-readable representation of our object's state. Whenever our object is printed or 
      concatenated, Java will automatically call our custom version in place of the default. 
      The method header is always:
    </p>

    <program language="java">
      <code>
public String toString()
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      <xref ref="listing-point-tostring"/> shows the overridden <c>toString</c> method for <c>Point</c>:
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing-point-tostring">
      <caption>The <c>toString</c> method for the <c>Point</c> class</caption>
      <program language="java">
        <code>
    /**
     * Returns a string representation of this Point in (x, y) coordinate format.
     *
     * Precondition: None.
     * Postcondition: Returns a formatted String containing this Point's coordinates;
     *                the state of this Point is unchanged.
     *
     * @return a string formatted as "(x, y)" representing this point's coordinates
     */
    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "(" + this.x + ", " + this.y + ")";
    }
        </code>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      With this method in place, our concatenation statement now outputs:
    </p>

    <console>
      <output>
The value of p1 is: (3.0, 4.0)
      </output>
    </console>

    <p>
      For composite objects like <c>Rectangle</c>, we write a <em>deep <c>toString</c></em>, 
      incorporating the string representation of component objects:
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing-rectangle-tostring">
      <caption>The <c>toString</c> method for the <c>Rectangle</c> class</caption>
      <program language="java">
        <code>
    /**
     * Returns a string representation of this Rectangle, including its topLeft
     * corner coordinates, width, and height.
     *
     * Precondition: None (topLeft may be null or an initialized Point).
     * Postcondition: Returns a formatted String describing this Rectangle's state;
     *                the state of this Rectangle and its topLeft point are unchanged.
     *
     * @return a string formatted as "Rectangle[topLeft=(x, y), width=W, height=H]"
     */
    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Rectangle[topLeft=" + this.topLeft 
               + ", width=" + this.width 
               + ", height=" + this.height + "]";
    }
        </code>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      If we construct a <c>Rectangle</c> of width <c>10.0</c> and height <c>20.0</c> anchored 
      at coordinate <m>(3.0, 4.0)</m>, passing the rectangle to <c>System.out.println(rect)</c> 
      implicitly calls <c>rect.toString()</c>, printing:
    </p>

    <console>
      <output>
Rectangle[topLeft=(3.0, 4.0), width=10.0, height=20.0]
      </output>
    </console>

    <p>
      Notice a significant advantage of string concatenation: if <c>this.topLeft</c> happens 
      to be <c>null</c>, Java safely appends the text literal <c>"null"</c> rather than attempting 
      to call <c>toString()</c> on a null reference, preventing a <c>NullPointerException</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Whenever you need to inspect an object during debugging, or present its state to users 
      in a log or console table, overriding <c>toString</c> gives you immediate, convenient 
      text output across the entire Java language ecosystem.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>